				<div class="info">
					<span class="printuser avatarhover"><a href="http://www.wikidot.com/user:info/moley" onclick="WIKIDOT.page.listeners.userInfo(301944); return false;"  ><img class="small" src="http://www.wikidot.com/avatar.php?userid=301944&amp;amp;size=small&amp;amp;timestamp=1609387651" alt="Moley" style="background-image:url(http://www.wikidot.com/userkarma.php?u=301944)"/></a><a href="http://www.wikidot.com/user:info/moley" onclick="WIKIDOT.page.listeners.userInfo(301944); return false;" >Moley</a></span> <span class="odate time_1239231518 format_%25e%20%25b%20%25Y%2C%20%25H%3A%25M%7Cagohover">08 Apr 2009 22:58</span>
														</div>
			</div>
			<div class="content" id="post-content-443901">
									

<p>The reason that Magic works and is the biggest CCG out there is precisely because it's simple. People like to play games and have fun, remembering 400 rules to do so makes it a greater barrier to entry to play.</p>
<p>Out of Magic, V:tES and L5R, all created by Garfield to improve on different aspects of CCG design, I'd rank L5R my favorite because of it's complexity but I'm fucked if I can get newbies to play as most people don't want to spend 4 hours learning a game that might last 30 minutes. If you want something to be successful you need to get people playing it, and people like things to be understandable and easy to learn. You can make the topend of the game rediculously complex but you need that inital "I can win by playing 682 really fucking quick and eating everything." to get people playing.</p>
<p>In regards to what you want to achieve if you manage it you'll be a millionare as EVERY game designer wants exactly that but you'll still not be able to stop people from making combo decks to win, short of saying "Your deck may only contain this card and this card and this card." which will just piss people off. People want to win and they want to do it in the most guarenteed way possible so for a hypothetical example you need to drop SCP-682, who costs 10 and kills 2 Agents as an extra cost, SCP-076-2, who costs 10 and requires SCP-076-1 to play which costs 8, and Dr Kondraki, who costs 12.</p>
<p>So to win I need the following:<br />
2 Agents, the cheaper the better, in my hand. Lets say they cost 2 each.<br />
Dr Kondraki in my hand.<br />
SCP-076-1 in my hand.<br />
SCP-076-2 in my hand.<br />
SCP-682 in my hand.<br />
44 Resources.</p>
<p>So my goal now is to get those things ASAP at the expense of everything else. I can dress it up to make it look like I'm bluffing and feinting when acctually all I'm doing is fishing for those cards. But if I have to dance around 30 or so restrictions to do it I'll just add that to the list of things I need to win, but if something easier comes up I'll just do that.</p>
<p>I don't want to sound like a total ass but I've worked on CCGs before and for the most part the ones designed with complexity as the core component just die. And in a previous post you said that people who play won't be there to just 'play cards' but they will, people will play the game for fun and anything that detracts from it will make them less and less likely to play.</p>

							</div>

								
					
