				<div class="info">
					<span class="printuser avatarhover"><a href="http://www.wikidot.com/user:info/drclef" onclick="WIKIDOT.page.listeners.userInfo(213153); return false;"  ><img class="small" src="http://www.wikidot.com/avatar.php?userid=213153&amp;amp;size=small&amp;amp;timestamp=1609387651" alt="DrClef" style="background-image:url(http://www.wikidot.com/userkarma.php?u=213153)"/></a><a href="http://www.wikidot.com/user:info/drclef" onclick="WIKIDOT.page.listeners.userInfo(213153); return false;" >DrClef</a></span> <span class="odate time_1239235752 format_%25e%20%25b%20%25Y%2C%20%25H%3A%25M%7Cagohover">09 Apr 2009 00:09</span>
														</div>
			</div>
			<div class="content" id="post-content-443941">
									

<p>One of the interesting things about complexity in games is that it comes in two different forms: I call them Rules Complexity and Emergent Complexity.</p>
<p>Rules Complexity is when the rules for playing the game are complex. Example: most Tabletop strategy games utilize Rules Complexity. There are rules for moving, rules for attacking, rules for how to measure range and distance, rules for resolving attacks, and so on.</p>
<p>Emergent Complexity happens when the way of winning the game requires subtlety and nuance. The classic example is Chess, but I prefer Go: the rules for Go are incredibly simple (one kind of piece, one type of move, one way to capture) but because of the scope of the game, you can wind up with incredibly deep gameplay.</p>
<p>Successful games rely more on Emergent Complexity than Rules Complexity: even a game like Magic, which is fairly complex in terms of Rules, utilizes Emergent Complexity to a great extent. The cornercases, the keywords, the various rule breakers, the way they interact, those get mindbendingly complex, but the basic game itself is deceptively simple: draw card, lay land, tap mana, cast spells, attack with creatures, reduce opponent to 0 life.</p>
<p>This game seems to have too much of Rules Complexity for my taste.</p>

							</div>

								
					
