					<span class="printuser avatarhover"><a href="http://www.wikidot.com/user:info/drclef" onclick="WIKIDOT.page.listeners.userInfo(213153); return false;"  ><img class="small" src="http://www.wikidot.com/avatar.php?userid=213153&amp;amp;size=small&amp;amp;timestamp=1609387651" alt="DrClef" style="background-image:url(http://www.wikidot.com/userkarma.php?u=213153)"/></a><a href="http://www.wikidot.com/user:info/drclef" onclick="WIKIDOT.page.listeners.userInfo(213153); return false;" >DrClef</a></span> <span class="odate time_1239256044 format_%25e%20%25b%20%25Y%2C%20%25H%3A%25M%7Cagohover">09 Apr 2009 05:47</span>
														</div>
			</div>
			<div class="content" id="post-content-444167">
									

<p>Thoughts.</p>
<p>1. Multiple token types for Personnel and D-Classes is needlessly complex. Consider this possibility: in TCGs like Duel Masters and Maple Story, a card can either be played as-is, or used as a resource instead. This would add an extra layer of strategy: do you play Clef's Shotgun as a weapon, to improve your personnel, or do you play it upside down for the extra D-Class personnel you get?</p>
<p>2. Using D-Class as cards allows you to use a tap-style system instead of tracking them as counters, since your D-Class reset at the end of each turn anyway.</p>
<p>3. Mission-Based gameplay reminds me a lot of the old Star Trek TCG. If you wanna talk about broken games… basically if you bought the Enterprise crew and set up the right missions, you were unstoppable. I'd prefer that Mission cards work like Alternate Win Condition cards in Magic, instead: you need to draw the card before you can play it.</p>

							</div>

								
					
