					<span class="printuser avatarhover"><a href="http://www.wikidot.com/user:info/agent-circeus" onclick="WIKIDOT.page.listeners.userInfo(276761); return false;"  ><img class="small" src="http://www.wikidot.com/avatar.php?userid=276761&amp;amp;size=small&amp;amp;timestamp=1609387651" alt="Agent Circeus" style="background-image:url(http://www.wikidot.com/userkarma.php?u=276761)"/></a><a href="http://www.wikidot.com/user:info/agent-circeus" onclick="WIKIDOT.page.listeners.userInfo(276761); return false;" >Agent Circeus</a></span> <span class="odate time_1239256194 format_%25e%20%25b%20%25Y%2C%20%25H%3A%25M%7Cagohover">09 Apr 2009 05:49</span>
														</div>
			</div>
			<div class="content" id="post-content-444169">
									

<p>Also, missions could be based on the HP CCG mechanic, but instead of hampering the opponent, they hamper and can be solved by both players. This becomes interesting because you have to balance between the benefit of playing the card and the risk of the other player completing it.</p>
<p>In any case, I believe missions, however we do them (they could just as well end up as the equivalent of "spells", after all!), should probably be just another type of card you can choose to integrate in a deck rather than a central element of the gameplay.</p>

							</div>

								
