														</div>
			</div>
			<div class="content" id="post-content-444388">
									

<p>Using cards as Class-D ressource is a great idea, but it may mess the table a little (having 15 card piled up, tapped, untaped…). Maybe placing them face down on the set, like Energy cards in Pokemon TCG would solve this little problem. It's a difficult sacrifice to place good cards as Class-D when your deck is perfect.</p>
<p>We can simplify the mission aspect of the game, indeed, because it's not a capital part of the backgroud (reports are more important from my point of view). The problem is, missions must not be prepared and awaited, played at the right time so only you can fulfill it (that's the problem in HP TCG : quidditch matches ruined the game). Drawing/playing a mission must be a dramatic event, or sometime a right-o-time escape plan. Lets say this : you have a crypt" of mission, a little secondary deck of 10 or 12 mission cards that you can draw/play anytime during your turn BUT, you have to play it immediately when you draw it. We keep the "both of you and opponent can do the mission" thing. So you can choose a set of mission that correspond to your deck, but you won't always draw what you need/expect. XK-class mission could then put an end to the game, so you'll build your deck around placing everything on the table to fix the Broken God and win the game. Though, a "Broken God" deck sounds a bit Yugioh to me.</p>
<p>I still believe complexicity is a necessity. When players will play this game, they must know and understand what's happenning, and the best thing to test this is : can they write a report of the event happening in the game (like Chronicles for VTES) : if they can't that means the game is too "virtual" and not "simulating" enough.</p>

							</div>

								
					
